const unit = (title, paragraphs, rows, terms, steps, trap, question, answer, mcq) => ({
 title, sections:paragraphs.map(([heading,text])=>({heading,text})), comparison:{headers:['Concept','Distinction','Application'],rows},
 terms:terms.map(([term,meaning])=>({term,meaning})), timelineTitle:'Reason through the topic', timeline:steps.map(([date,event])=>({date,event})), trap,
 practice:{question,answer}, mcq
});

export const units = {
 geography:[
 unit('Physical geography: from a process to a landscape',[
 ['Start with energy and movement','Physical geography explains how energy and matter move through the Earth system. Internal energy drives tectonic activity, while solar energy powers atmospheric circulation and much of the water cycle. A landscape records interacting processes over time. Describe the process before naming its resulting landform: weathering breaks material down, erosion removes it, and deposition occurs when transport capacity falls.'],
 ['Read diagrams as explanations','A diagram should show direction and relationships, not just labels. For plate boundaries, indicate relative motion; for winds, distinguish pressure-gradient force from the Coriolis effect; for rainfall, show the mechanism lifting moist air. A coastal location alone does not guarantee heavy rainfall. Relief, prevailing winds, currents and seasonal circulation help explain differences between places at similar latitudes.'],
 ['Connect maps with scale','Latitude, elevation, distance from the sea and local relief operate at different scales. Use them together rather than treating one factor as a complete explanation. On a map, identify hemisphere and position before inferring climate. In an answer, explain a causal chain and then qualify it with an exception. This method also helps distinguish a long-term climatic pattern from one weather event.']
 ],[['Weathering','Breakdown in place','Do not assume transport has occurred.'],['Erosion','Removal and transport','Agents include water, wind and ice.'],['Deposition','Accumulation of transported material','Relate it to changing transport capacity.'],['Weather / climate','Short-term conditions / longer-term patterns','One unusual day does not establish a climatic trend.']], [['Relief','Variation in elevation and terrain.'],['Insolation','Incoming solar radiation.'],['Drainage basin','An area whose runoff feeds a common river system.']], [['Locate','Mark latitude, elevation and neighbouring physical features.'],['Explain','Identify energy, forces and movement.'],['Qualify','Check local conditions and the scale of the claim.']], 'The Coriolis effect deflects motion; it does not by itself initiate wind.', 'Why can opposite slopes of a mountain receive different rainfall?', 'Moist air rising on a windward slope cools and can condense. Air descending on the leeward side warms and may be drier. Explain prevailing wind direction and moisture supply before applying the rain-shadow model.', ['Which process necessarily involves removal of material?',['Weathering alone','Erosion','Condensation','Insolation'],1,'Erosion includes removal and usually transport. Weathering is breakdown at the site.']),
 unit('Indian geography: regions, resources and hazards',[
 ['Build a spatial framework','Place the Himalayan system, northern plains, peninsular plateau, coastal plains and islands on a blank map. Then connect each region with its underlying structure, rivers and broad climatic setting. Physiographic regions are analytical categories; their boundaries do not always follow state borders. A political map and a physical map answer different questions and should be used together.'],
 ['Explain the monsoon carefully','Seasonal changes in heating and circulation are central to the monsoon, but a single land–sea breeze analogy is incomplete. Relief, ocean conditions and the migration of rain-bearing systems affect onset, distribution and breaks. Distinguish seasonal totals from timing: a normal total can still conceal damaging dry spells or intense short events. This distinction matters for farming and flood management.'],
 ['Connect Bihar to the basin','North Bihar is linked to rivers descending from the Himalayas and to a wider transboundary basin. Flood risk depends on rainfall, sediment, river movement, drainage, settlements and preparedness. Embankments can alter risk but do not remove every hazard. A strong answer combines physical processes with exposure and vulnerability rather than calling all flooding simply a natural event.']
 ],[['Himalayan rivers','Large upstream catchments and sediment loads','Use basin-scale explanations.'],['Peninsular rivers','Diverse plateau and rainfall settings','Avoid treating every river as identical.'],['Hazard','A potentially damaging event','Risk also depends on people and assets exposed.'],['Resource','Something useful under particular conditions','Technology and demand can change its value.']], [['Catchment','Land contributing water to a drainage outlet.'],['Vulnerability','Susceptibility to harm and limited capacity to cope.'],['Alluvium','Material deposited by running water.']], [['Map','Locate the region, river and downstream connections.'],['Trace','Follow water, sediment and human settlement.'],['Respond','Combine prevention, preparedness and recovery.']], 'High rainfall and high flood risk are related, but they are not interchangeable measures.', 'Why is flood planning stronger at basin scale than at one district boundary?', 'Water and sediment move across administrative borders. Upstream rainfall, reservoirs, land use and channel changes affect downstream districts, requiring coordination alongside local warning, drainage and evacuation measures.', ['Which combination best explains flood risk?',['Rainfall alone','Hazard, exposure and vulnerability','Population alone','River length alone'],1,'A physical event creates different losses depending on who and what is exposed and how vulnerable they are.']),
 unit('Human geography: location, population and development',[
 ['Look beyond population totals','Distribution, density, age structure and migration describe different aspects of population. A dense region may have good services, while a sparsely populated area may face severe access problems. Compare denominators and dates before interpreting statistics. Migration can be seasonal, circular or permanent; a simple rural-to-urban story misses movements among rural areas, cities and regions.'],
 ['Understand location decisions','Agriculture, manufacturing and services locate according to different combinations of land, labour, transport, markets, energy and institutions. Improvements in connectivity may change these relationships without making distance irrelevant. Industrial clusters can benefit from suppliers and skills, but they may also face congestion and pollution. Explain advantages and costs instead of calling concentration inherently good or bad.'],
 ['Measure development broadly','Income is important but does not directly measure health, education, agency or environmental quality. Human development asks what people can actually do and be. Regional averages can hide inequalities among households and social groups. For Bihar, connect migration, agriculture, urban services and skills through mechanisms; avoid using a single ranking as a complete explanation of the state’s economy or society.']
 ],[['Population growth','Change in a population over time','Not the same as its density.'],['Migration','Movement across places','Analyse origin and destination effects.'],['Agglomeration','Benefits from clustering activities','Balance them against congestion costs.'],['Human development','Expansion of capabilities','Income is a means as well as an outcome.']], [['Density','Population relative to area.'],['Urbanisation','A growing urban share or expansion of urban ways and spaces.'],['Accessibility','Ease of reaching opportunities or services.']], [['Describe','Use a map and clearly defined indicators.'],['Explain','Connect location, livelihoods and institutions.'],['Evaluate','Ask who benefits and who bears the costs.']], 'A higher average income does not show that every resident is better off.', 'How can migration benefit both the place of origin and the destination?', 'Remittances and skills can support origin households, while workers contribute at destinations. Discuss costs too: insecure work, separation, pressure on services and unequal access to protection.', ['Which measure directly relates population to land area?',['Growth rate','Population density','Life expectancy','Sex ratio'],1,'Density uses area as its denominator. The other measures describe different dimensions.'])
 ],
 polity:[
 unit('Constitutional foundations: authority with limits',[
 ['Why a constitution matters','A constitution establishes institutions, distributes power and expresses commitments about political life. Constitutionalism adds the idea that power should be limited and accountable. A written document alone cannot guarantee that idea in practice. Ask how rules, courts, representation and public conduct make authority answerable, and how citizens can seek a remedy when a commitment is violated.'],
 ['Connect rights and social conditions','Formal equality means little if people cannot access institutions or challenge discrimination. Distinguish equal legal status from measures intended to address disadvantage. Fundamental rights, directive principles and duties have different constitutional roles; they should not be treated as one interchangeable list. Use the official constitutional text to check wording and provisions rather than relying on a remembered slogan.'],
 ['Explain democracy beyond an election','Elections enable selection and removal of representatives, but democracy also needs public reasoning, accountability, liberties and participation between elections. Diversity does not automatically lead to conflict: institutions can accommodate differences through representation and power-sharing. A good answer explains how a mechanism works and recognises its limitations instead of merely calling an institution democratic.']
 ],[['Constitution','Basic institutional and normative framework','Read the text and context.'],['Constitutionalism','Limits on arbitrary power','A question of practice as well as form.'],['Equality','Equal status and protection','Examine barriers to effective access.'],['Representation','Acting politically on behalf of others','Accountability must continue after election.']], [['Rule of law','Public power exercised through law rather than arbitrary will.'],['Liberty','Protected freedom subject to the constitutional framework.'],['Secularism','An approach to state–religion relations and equal citizenship.']], [['Identify','Which value or constitutional provision is involved?'],['Distinguish','What kind of obligation or protection does it create?'],['Apply','How does it affect the concrete situation?']], 'Having elections is necessary for electoral democracy but does not settle every question about its quality.', 'How can equal treatment and support for disadvantaged groups be compatible?', 'Identical treatment can preserve unequal starting conditions. Carefully designed measures can improve substantive access while remaining subject to constitutional justification, limits and review.', ['Which statement best describes constitutionalism?',['A very long constitution','Government power limited by an accountable framework','Rule by any elected majority without limits','Only the existence of courts'],1,'Constitutionalism concerns limits and accountability, not merely the length or written form of a document.']),
 unit('Institutions: separate functions, shared accountability',[
 ['Follow the decision through institutions','Instead of memorising an office in isolation, trace how a public decision is proposed, authorised, implemented and reviewed. Legislatures deliberate and authorise; executives administer; courts decide legal disputes and review according to their jurisdiction. Their functions overlap in defined ways. The presence of overlap does not mean there are no limits or that each institution can assume every role.'],
 ['Distinguish responsibility and independence','Political executives in a parliamentary system are answerable through legislative mechanisms, while permanent administration operates under a different set of responsibilities. Judicial independence protects adjudication from improper pressure, but is not a claim that judicial institutions are beyond all accountability. Explain the particular mechanism—questions, committees, financial scrutiny or reasoned judgments—rather than simply praising checks and balances.'],
 ['Read federal relations in context','Federalism distributes authority across levels of government. Legislative competence, administrative cooperation and fiscal resources are distinct dimensions. A function assigned on paper may be difficult to perform without staff or funds. Conflicts should be analysed through institutional arrangements and constitutional procedures. Verify current provisions and judgments before adding contemporary legal claims to an exam answer.']
 ],[['Legislature','Deliberation, law and scrutiny','Different procedures serve different purposes.'],['Executive','Implementation and political direction','Separate temporary leadership from permanent administration.'],['Judiciary','Adjudication and legal review','Jurisdiction and remedies matter.'],['Federalism','Distribution of authority across levels','Finance affects practical autonomy.']], [['Collective responsibility','A shared political responsibility of a council of ministers within the parliamentary framework.'],['Judicial review','Review of public action against applicable legal standards.'],['Delegation','Authority entrusted for a defined purpose within limits.']], [['Authority','Identify the competent institution.'],['Procedure','Check how the decision is made.'],['Review','Identify scrutiny, remedies and accountability.']], 'Institutional independence and absence of accountability are not synonyms.', 'Why can a legislative committee add value beyond a floor debate?', 'Committees can examine detailed evidence, question officials and scrutinise specific subjects with continuity. Their value depends on information, deliberation, follow-up and the powers available to them.', ['Which approach best examines an institution?',['Only learn its office-holder','Trace powers, procedures and accountability','Assume independence means unlimited power','Ignore how it is funded'],1,'Institutions are understood through what they can do, how they act and how their actions are scrutinised.']),
 unit('Governance: turn a right into a working service',[
 ['The implementation chain','A policy announcement is not the same as service delivery. Trace eligibility, application, verification, decision, payment or provision, and grievance resolution. Each step may exclude people through distance, documentation, language or discretion. Distinguish the legal promise, administrative design and actual outcome. An effective answer specifies where the failure occurs before proposing a reform.'],
 ['Local government and participation','Decentralisation can improve responsiveness when functions, funds and staff support one another. Merely creating an elected local body does not ensure meaningful authority. Participation should include people who have less time, literacy or social influence. Public meetings, disclosure and social audit can help, but their effectiveness depends on access to information and a response to the issues raised.'],
 ['Technology is one part of the system','Digitisation may reduce repeated paperwork and improve tracking, but can also create exclusion when authentication fails or connectivity is poor. Build an assisted or offline route and an appeal mechanism into the design. Accountability requires responsibility and correction, not only a dashboard. Use a citizen’s experience to connect institutional rules with measurable outcomes.']
 ],[['Output','An activity or service produced','For example, applications processed.'],['Outcome','A change experienced by people','For example, improved access to the intended benefit.'],['Transparency','Information made accessible','Does not automatically produce corrective action.'],['Accountability','Answerability and consequences or correction','Needs a functioning response mechanism.']], [['Grievance redress','A process for resolving complaints and correcting failures.'],['Social audit','Public examination of implementation with community participation.'],['Decentralisation','Transfer or distribution of functions and authority towards lower levels.']], [['Map','Follow a citizen through the service.'],['Diagnose','Locate the source of delay or exclusion.'],['Repair','Assign action, review and an appeal route.']], 'A digital service is not accessible merely because it exists online.', 'A beneficiary is eligible but online authentication repeatedly fails. What should a service design provide?', 'An authorised assisted or alternative verification route, a recorded complaint, time-bound review and protection against arbitrary denial. The objective is both integrity of delivery and meaningful access.', ['Which indicator is an outcome rather than a processing output?',['Number of forms uploaded','Number of meetings held','Reduction in eligible households denied a benefit','Number of circulars issued'],2,'An outcome measures a change in people’s experience rather than only administrative activity.'])
 ],
 economy:[
 unit('Economic reasoning: choices, incentives and measurement',[
 ['Begin with scarcity and alternatives','An economic choice uses resources that could have been used elsewhere. Opportunity cost is the value of the best forgone alternative, not every possible alternative added together. Separate positive analysis of what may happen from a normative judgment about what should happen. A model simplifies reality so that a relationship can be examined; always state what is being held constant.'],
 ['Markets need careful diagrams','Demand describes a relationship between price and quantity demanded under specified conditions. A change in the good’s own price is a movement along a demand curve; a change in income or preferences can shift it. Supply, production costs and market structure affect the outcome too. Equilibrium is a model of compatible plans, not a guarantee of fairness or a claim that every real market instantly clears.'],
 ['Read the numbers critically','A percentage change needs a starting value. An average may hide variation, and correlation alone does not establish a causal mechanism. Ask how data were collected, what the unit is and which population is represented. In an answer, use the indicator that matches the question rather than inserting any available statistic. This is especially important when comparing incomes, prices and living standards.']
 ],[['Opportunity cost','Best forgone alternative','Use a concrete choice to explain it.'],['Demand shift','Relationship changes','Different from movement caused by own price.'],['Mean','Arithmetic average','Can be affected by extreme values.'],['Correlation','Co-movement','Does not by itself establish causation.']], [['Marginal','The change associated with one additional unit.'],['Elasticity','Responsiveness measured relative to a change in another variable.'],['Externality','An effect on others not fully reflected in the transaction.']], [['Define','Identify the choice and relevant variables.'],['Model','State assumptions and trace the relationship.'],['Check','Test limits, evidence and distributional effects.']], 'A model’s equilibrium is not automatically a socially desirable outcome.', 'Why could average income rise without improving the position of most households?', 'Large gains concentrated in a small group can raise the mean. Examine the distribution, median, employment and living costs before inferring a broad improvement.', ['What is opportunity cost?',['All money previously spent','The best alternative forgone','Every alternative added together','Only accounting profit'],1,'It is the value of the next-best feasible option that was sacrificed.']),
 unit('Macroeconomics: output, money and public finance',[
 ['Keep flows and stocks separate','National income accounts measure economic activity over a period, while a stock is measured at a point in time. GDP records production within a territory; household well-being depends on more than aggregate output. Distinguish nominal changes from changes adjusted for prices. Transfers and financial transactions do not all represent newly produced goods and services.'],
 ['Trace monetary transmission','Banks, borrowers, savers and payment systems connect finance to the real economy. A policy-rate change can influence market rates, credit conditions and spending, but transmission depends on balance sheets, expectations and other conditions. Inflation is a rise in a price index over time; its sources can involve demand, supply and expectations. Avoid describing every episode with one universal cause.'],
 ['Read budgets as linked accounts','Government receipts and spending have different categories. A deficit is a flow during a period; accumulated debt is a stock shaped by past borrowing and other changes. Borrowing can support productive investment or finance less durable expenditure, so the use of funds and future obligations both matter. Check the latest official document before quoting current fiscal numbers or policy settings.']
 ],[['Nominal / real','Current prices / adjusted for price change','Necessary for comparing output over time.'],['Deficit / debt','Period flow / accumulated stock','Do not interchange the terms.'],['Inflation','Change in a price index','A falling rate can still mean rising prices.'],['Policy rate','One monetary-policy instrument','Effects depend on transmission.']], [['GDP','Value of final production within an economy over a period.'],['Fiscal policy','Government decisions about revenue, spending and borrowing.'],['Monetary transmission','Channels through which policy affects financial conditions and activity.']], [['Account','Define what is measured and over which period.'],['Transmit','Follow effects through institutions and incentives.'],['Evaluate','Consider timing, distribution and trade-offs.']], 'Disinflation is a slower rise in prices; it is not necessarily a fall in the price level.', 'Why might a lower policy rate not immediately create more investment?', 'Banks may not fully pass it on, firms may lack demand or confidence, and debt or risk can constrain borrowers. Explain the transmission chain rather than assuming a one-step relationship.', ['Which is normally a stock measure?',['Annual deficit','Monthly income','Public debt at a specified date','Quarterly production'],2,'Debt at a date is a stock; income, production and deficits are measured over periods.']),
 unit('Development: jobs, capabilities and structural change',[
 ['Growth and development answer different questions','Growth expands output, while development also concerns capabilities, distribution and structural change. A sector can produce a large share of output without employing an equally large share of workers. Examine productivity and job quality rather than assuming that any increase in GDP automatically creates sufficient secure employment. Education and health contribute both directly to well-being and indirectly to productive capacity.'],
 ['Connect rural and urban economies','Agricultural productivity, storage, transport, non-farm work and migration shape household opportunities. Rural development is not limited to farm output. Credit, water, skills and institutions interact; a road without a functioning market or a loan without viable demand may have limited effects. For Bihar, explain how seasonal migration and local livelihoods connect instead of treating them as completely separate systems.'],
 ['Trade-offs and evidence','Infrastructure and industry can generate benefits while displacing people or imposing environmental costs. Evaluate who receives gains, who bears risks and whether safeguards are effective. Global integration creates opportunities and exposure to external shocks. Use dated official data and distinguish a policy’s stated objective from evidence of its results. Avoid using one national average as a complete account of all regions.']
 ],[['Growth','Increase in output','Not a complete measure of well-being.'],['Human capital','Capabilities supporting productive activity','Health and education also have intrinsic value.'],['Informality','Work or enterprises outside parts of formal protection and regulation','Not identical to unemployment.'],['Structural change','Changing sectoral organisation','Examine movement of workers as well as output.']], [['Productivity','Output relative to an input.'],['Remittance','Resources sent by a migrant to another household or place.'],['Inclusive development','Development attentive to participation and distribution of benefits.']], [['Describe','Compare sectors, jobs and capabilities.'],['Connect','Trace infrastructure, markets and institutions.'],['Assess','Measure outcomes and distributional costs.']], 'Being employed does not establish that a person has adequate income or social protection.', 'How can rising agricultural productivity support development beyond agriculture?', 'It can improve incomes, release resources, create demand and supply inputs, but the outcome depends on distribution, non-farm opportunities and the ability of workers to move into productive employment.', ['Which best distinguishes development from output growth alone?',['Only a larger GDP total','Changes in capabilities, distribution and opportunities as well as output','Only higher exports','Only more urban land'],1,'Development includes qualitative and distributive changes that an aggregate output measure cannot capture.'])
 ],
 science:[
 unit('Physical science: explain before memorising',[
 ['Use quantities precisely','Force, energy, power, heat and temperature describe different things. A correct numerical answer needs units and a clearly defined system. Conservation laws help track transfers instead of treating energy as something that simply disappears. Friction can convert organised motion into internal energy; that does not mean the total energy has vanished.'],
 ['Models connect observations','Atomic and molecular models explain chemical reactions and material properties at a scale that cannot be inferred from appearance alone. A physical change and a chemical change are distinguished by what happens to substances, not merely whether a visible change occurs. Balance a reaction by conserving atoms rather than changing the chemical identity of a formula to make arithmetic easier.'],
 ['From principle to application','For light, draw the path and identify the interface. For electricity, identify the circuit and distinguish potential difference, current and resistance. For heat transfer, separate conduction, convection and radiation. Relating a principle to an appliance or environmental situation helps reveal misunderstandings that memorising a definition can conceal. Keep safety-related examples conceptual rather than giving instructions for experiments with mains electricity or hazardous chemicals.']
 ],[['Energy / power','Capacity or transfer / rate of transfer','A powerful device does not necessarily run for longer.'],['Heat / temperature','Energy transfer / measure related to thermal state','Objects at the same temperature can contain different internal energies.'],['Current / voltage','Charge flow rate / potential difference','They are not interchangeable.'],['Physical / chemical change','State or arrangement / chemical composition','Visible change alone is not decisive.']], [['Conservation','A quantity remains accounted for within the defined system and exchanges.'],['Refraction','Change in propagation direction associated with a change in medium.'],['Resistance','Opposition to current under specified conditions.']], [['Define','State the system, variable and units.'],['Explain','Apply a law or model.'],['Test','Check whether the result is physically reasonable.']], 'Power and energy have different units and meanings.', 'Why can a small high-power device use less energy than a low-power device?', 'Energy depends on power and operating time. A brief high-power use may consume less total energy than a lower-power device operating for many hours.', ['Which quantity describes a rate of energy transfer?',['Power','Mass','Distance','Electric charge'],0,'Power is energy transferred per unit time.']),
 unit('Life science: structure, function and evidence',[
 ['Organise levels of explanation','Cells, tissues, organs and organ systems describe different levels of organisation. A structure’s function should be explained through what it does and how it interacts with other parts. Plants and animals both need energy and regulation, but their mechanisms differ. Avoid assuming that a familiar human process is the only way living organisms perform a function.'],
 ['Connect inheritance and variation','Heredity concerns transmission of biological information; variation arises through several processes and interacts with the environment. A trait can have multiple influences, so a simple classroom inheritance example should not be applied to every complex characteristic. Evolution concerns changes in populations over generations, not an individual changing because it wishes to adapt.'],
 ['Health concepts require distinctions','A pathogen, a symptom and a risk factor are different categories. Infectious and non-infectious conditions do not all respond to the same intervention. Vaccination, sanitation, nutrition and surveillance act through different mechanisms. These lessons concern scientific concepts and public systems; use authoritative health sources for current recommendations rather than extending a school-level explanation into personal treatment advice.']
 ],[['Cell / tissue','Basic unit / organised group','Connect structure to a specific function.'],['Heredity / variation','Transmission / differences','Both are relevant to population change.'],['Pathogen / symptom','Causal agent / observed effect','An effect does not identify its cause by itself.'],['Immunity / treatment','Defence mechanisms / intervention after or during illness','Different roles should not be conflated.']], [['Homeostasis','Regulation of internal conditions.'],['Gene','A unit of hereditary information in biological context.'],['Population','Members of a species considered as a group in an area or system.']], [['Observe','Describe the structure or process.'],['Connect','Relate it to function and regulation.'],['Evaluate','Check evidence and avoid overgeneralisation.']], 'Evolution occurs across populations and generations, not as a purposeful change made by an individual.', 'Why should a single-gene classroom example not explain every human characteristic?', 'Many traits involve multiple genetic influences and environmental interactions. The simplified model illustrates a mechanism but does not establish that all traits follow the same pattern.', ['Which describes evolution most accurately?',['An individual deciding to change','Changes in population characteristics across generations','Only growth in body size','Any temporary adjustment'],1,'Evolution concerns heritable population-level change across generations.']),
 unit('Applied science: technology, benefits and limitations',[
 ['Separate a tool from its application','Remote sensing gathers information from a distance, while navigation systems estimate position and timing. Satellites can carry different instruments in different orbits, so the word satellite does not identify a single function. Explain the physical principle and then the use case. For agriculture or disasters, connect the observation to a decision and identify the limits of resolution, timing and interpretation.'],
 ['Understand a technology chain','Biotechnology, semiconductors and digital systems each depend on a sequence of inputs, processes and validation. A laboratory demonstration does not automatically imply an affordable, reliable product at scale. Testing, manufacturing, maintenance and access shape public benefit. Avoid describing a promising technique as a guaranteed solution to every problem in its field.'],
 ['Evaluate evidence and governance','A technology answer needs both mechanism and consequences. Discuss reliability, resource demands, privacy, access and possible misuse where relevant. Artificial-intelligence outputs can contain errors even when fluent; nuclear applications differ between energy, medicine and other fields. Use current official institutional sources for mission details or operational status, while keeping the scientific foundation separate from rapidly changing announcements.']
 ],[['Remote sensing','Observation at a distance','Data still require interpretation.'],['Navigation','Position and timing services','Not the same as imaging.'],['Prototype / deployment','Demonstration / operational use','Scaling and reliability intervene.'],['Accuracy / usefulness','Performance measure / fit for purpose','A useful system also needs accessibility and governance.']], [['Resolution','Ability to distinguish spatial, temporal or other detail.'],['Validation','Testing whether a method meets its intended requirements.'],['Semiconductor','A material whose electrical properties can be controlled for electronic uses.']], [['Mechanism','Explain what the technology does.'],['Application','Connect it to a real public problem.'],['Limits','Evaluate reliability, access and safeguards.']], 'A technology’s potential benefit is not the same as evidence that it has already delivered that benefit at scale.', 'How can satellite images help flood management without replacing local knowledge?', 'They can reveal inundation and changes over large areas, while local reports identify people, access routes and conditions not captured in an image. Combine timeliness, resolution and ground verification.', ['Which statement is most accurate?',['Every satellite takes photographs','Remote-sensing data may need ground verification','A prototype is always ready for mass deployment','More data automatically remove uncertainty'],1,'Observation and interpretation can have limits; ground information helps validate and contextualise remote data.'])
 ],
 environment:[
 unit('Ecology: relationships, flows and diversity',[
 ['An ecosystem is a set of interactions','Organisms interact with one another and with physical conditions. Producers, consumers and decomposers describe functional roles, not a rigid ranking of importance. Food webs are more realistic than isolated chains because many organisms use several resources. Removing one component can have indirect effects, but their scale depends on the network and local conditions.'],
 ['Energy and matter behave differently','Energy enters many ecosystems through photosynthesis and is dissipated through successive transfers; nutrients can cycle among living organisms, soil, water and the atmosphere. Do not say that energy is recycled in the same way as matter. Ecological pyramids depend on what is being measured, while simple rules of thumb should not be treated as identical observations for every ecosystem.'],
 ['Diversity has several dimensions','Genetic, species and ecosystem diversity describe different levels. Richness counts species, while the balance of their abundance also affects diversity. An endemic species has a restricted geographic distribution; that does not automatically mean it is threatened. Assess habitat, pressures and population evidence separately. This distinction is useful when reading conservation claims and species-based questions.']
 ],[['Energy flow','Transfer with dissipation','Not a closed recycling loop.'],['Nutrient cycle','Movement among reservoirs','Connect biological and physical processes.'],['Endemic','Restricted distribution','Not automatically an extinction-risk category.'],['Food web','Interconnected feeding relationships','Indirect effects can matter.']], [['Habitat','The environment in which an organism lives.'],['Niche','An organism’s ecological role and relationships.'],['Trophic level','A position in feeding relationships.']], [['Identify','List living and non-living components.'],['Trace','Follow energy, nutrients and interactions.'],['Predict','Consider direct and indirect effects of change.']], 'Endemic, invasive and endangered describe different properties.', 'Why might the loss of one species affect organisms that do not feed directly on it?', 'Food webs include indirect relationships through predators, competitors, resources and habitat modification. Explain a chain of effects and acknowledge that real systems can respond differently.', ['Which is generally recycled through an ecosystem?',['Energy in exactly the same form','Nutrients such as carbon and nitrogen','All incoming sunlight','Every organism indefinitely'],1,'Matter cycles among reservoirs, while usable energy is progressively dissipated.']),
 unit('Pollution and climate: sources, pathways and impacts',[
 ['Trace the exposure pathway','A pollutant matters through its source, transport, transformation and contact with living systems. Concentration, duration and vulnerability affect harm. A clear river is not necessarily free of dissolved contamination, and dilution does not remove the total pollutant load. Compare prevention at source with treatment after release rather than treating all waste management as an end-of-pipe problem.'],
 ['Distinguish related atmospheric issues','The greenhouse effect concerns the balance of incoming and outgoing energy. Ozone depletion involves a different atmospheric process, although some chemicals connect the issues. Climate is evaluated through longer-term patterns and multiple observations, not a single hot or cold day. Mitigation addresses the drivers of change; adaptation reduces harm from experienced or expected effects.'],
 ['Choose responses at the right scale','Household action, municipal services, industrial regulation and international cooperation act at different levels. A response can shift pollution elsewhere if its full life cycle is ignored. Evaluate trade-offs, monitoring and implementation. For Bihar, water quality, waste handling, flood exposure and agricultural practices connect ecological conditions with health and livelihoods; avoid blaming outcomes on individual behaviour alone.']
 ],[['Mitigation','Reduce drivers of climate change','For example, lowering emissions.'],['Adaptation','Reduce vulnerability to effects','For example, heat preparedness.'],['Concentration / load','Amount per volume / total amount','Dilution can lower one without removing the other.'],['Ozone depletion / warming','Different atmospheric processes','Do not use the terms interchangeably.']], [['Exposure','Contact with a hazard through a pathway.'],['Eutrophication','Nutrient enrichment that can alter aquatic systems.'],['Life cycle','Stages from resource extraction through use and disposal.']], [['Source','Identify where the pressure originates.'],['Pathway','Track movement and contact.'],['Response','Prevent, reduce, monitor and review.']], 'A lower concentration after dilution does not mean that the pollutant has ceased to exist.', 'Why should urban air-pollution policy consider more than private cars?', 'Industry, power, construction, dust, waste burning and other sources can contribute differently by location and season. Use source evidence to prioritise measures and assess exposure, enforcement and alternatives.', ['Which is primarily an adaptation measure?',['Reducing fuel emissions','Preparing heat-action and cooling-access plans','Capturing carbon emissions','Replacing a high-emission process'],1,'Heat preparedness reduces harm from climatic conditions; the other examples primarily reduce drivers.']),
 unit('Conservation: ecological outcomes and public institutions',[
 ['Protection involves people and places','A protected boundary is only one part of conservation. Connectivity, habitat quality, enforcement, livelihoods and the rights of affected communities influence results. A conservation category does not by itself reveal management quality. Examine ecological objectives and the institutional arrangements required to achieve them, and use official current sources for legal designations and changing status.'],
 ['Compare conservation strategies','In-situ conservation works within natural habitats; ex-situ approaches operate outside them, such as seed banks or managed breeding. They can complement one another but are not interchangeable. Restoring a degraded ecosystem is more complex than planting any trees: species composition, water, soil, local ecology and long-term maintenance matter. A plantation should not automatically be described as equivalent to a natural forest.'],
 ['Turn commitments into implementation','Environmental governance includes standards, assessment, monitoring, incentives and accountability. International commitments and domestic implementation are distinct steps. Evaluate whether institutions have data, capacity and a response when requirements are violated. For an exam answer, connect environmental protection with distributional questions: who benefits, who bears costs and how participation and remedies are organised.']
 ],[['In situ','Conservation within habitats','Protect ecological relationships.'],['Ex situ','Conservation outside natural habitats','Can preserve material or support recovery.'],['Plantation / natural forest','Planted stand / complex evolving ecosystem','Area alone does not capture ecological quality.'],['Commitment / outcome','Stated objective / measured result','Implementation and monitoring connect them.']], [['Connectivity','Links allowing ecological movement and interaction.'],['Restoration','Helping a degraded ecosystem recover relevant functions and characteristics.'],['Precaution','Considering serious uncertain risks before harm becomes established.']], [['Objective','Define the ecological outcome.'],['Design','Combine institutions, incentives and participation.'],['Monitor','Measure change and correct failures.']], 'Tree cover, forest quality and biodiversity are related but not identical indicators.', 'Why might a tree-planting target fail to restore an ecosystem?', 'Inappropriate species, poor survival, altered water use, loss of open habitats or lack of maintenance can undermine the objective. Define the original ecosystem and measure function and diversity, not only saplings planted.', ['Which is an ex-situ conservation example?',['Protecting a wild habitat','Maintaining a seed bank','Preserving a migration corridor','Reducing disturbance in a forest'],1,'A seed bank conserves biological material outside the natural habitat.'])
 ],
 ethics:[
 unit('Ethical reasoning: values, duties and consequences',[
 ['Clarify the moral question','An ethical problem is not solved merely by stating that honesty is good. Identify the decision, affected people and competing claims. Some dilemmas involve two defensible values, such as openness and privacy; others are clear misconduct presented as if it were a dilemma. Distinguish uncertainty about facts from disagreement about values before deciding how to act.'],
 ['Use more than one lens','A consequences-based approach asks about benefits and harms; a duty-based approach asks about obligations and constraints; a virtue perspective asks what character and judgment a responsible person should develop. These lenses can illuminate different parts of a case. They should not become names inserted without application, nor excuses for ignoring lawful authority or the dignity of people affected.'],
 ['Make principles operational','Explain the action a value requires. Integrity may require declaring a conflict; empathy may require listening before deciding; objectivity may require checking evidence consistently. Good intentions do not excuse avoidable harm or arbitrary methods. A strong answer shows how a principle guides a practical decision and how the decision will be communicated, recorded and reviewed.']
 ],[['Value','A quality or commitment considered important','Explain its practical implication.'],['Duty','An obligation attached to a role or relationship','Identify to whom it is owed.'],['Consequence','An effect of an action','Assess distribution and uncertainty.'],['Virtue','A cultivated disposition','Show it through conduct rather than praise.']], [['Moral dilemma','A conflict among serious moral claims.'],['Integrity','Consistency of conduct with justified commitments.'],['Empathy','Understanding another person’s perspective or experience.']], [['Clarify','Separate facts, assumptions and value conflicts.'],['Compare','Assess duties, consequences and affected people.'],['Justify','Choose and explain an accountable action.']], 'Calling a choice difficult does not make a plainly corrupt action ethically defensible.', 'An officer can help more people by bypassing a safeguard. How should the decision be examined?', 'Identify the safeguard’s purpose, the officer’s lawful discretion, risks to individuals and less harmful authorised alternatives. Seek an expedited legitimate route where possible and record reasons; do not assume that a claimed good outcome erases duties.', null),
 unit('Public-service ethics: fairness, probity and accountability',[
 ['Role obligations differ from private preference','Public authority is exercised on behalf of others. Personal sympathy or political loyalty cannot replace justified criteria. Impartiality means treating relevantly similar cases consistently, while recognising legally and ethically relevant differences. Non-partisanship concerns service to the constitutional office rather than a party. Neither value requires indifference to disadvantage or refusal to listen to citizens.'],
 ['Design against conflicts','A conflict of interest arises when a secondary interest could improperly influence a public duty. It can exist before any proven wrongdoing. Disclosure, recusal, independent review and clear records help manage it. A code of conduct supplies specific rules; a code of ethics articulates broader values. Organisational incentives and leadership matter because misconduct is not always prevented by issuing another circular.'],
 ['Accountability is a working relationship','Transparency makes information accessible; answerability requires reasons; correction addresses the failure. A service promise without a complaint route is weak. Protect relevant privacy while giving enough information for scrutiny. In case studies, explain how a decision will be documented, reviewed and followed up. This makes probity an administrative practice rather than a ceremonial statement of honesty.']
 ],[['Impartiality','Use relevant and consistent criteria','Not blind sameness in every situation.'],['Conflict of interest','A risk of improper influence','Can exist without proven corruption.'],['Conduct code','Specific expected or prohibited acts','Needs interpretation and enforcement.'],['Ethics code','Broader values and commitments','Does not replace operational rules.']], [['Probity','Integrity and propriety in the use of entrusted authority.'],['Recusal','Stepping aside from a decision because of a relevant conflict.'],['Answerability','An obligation to explain and justify conduct.']], [['Disclose','Identify relevant interests and constraints.'],['Decide','Use evidence, fair procedure and authorised discretion.'],['Review','Record reasons and provide correction mechanisms.']], 'A conflict of interest is not automatically proof of corruption, but ignoring it can undermine trust.', 'A relative’s firm qualifies for a tender evaluated by your team. What should you do?', 'Disclose the relationship through the required channel, avoid influencing the evaluation, seek recusal or independent review, and preserve a record. Do not secretly favour the firm or automatically punish it without a lawful basis.', null),
 unit('Case studies: choose, implement and learn',[
 ['Start with the facts that matter','List stakeholders and urgent risks, but do not spend the whole answer reproducing the story. Mark facts that are missing and explain what needs rapid verification. Distinguish immediate protection from a longer investigation. In a crisis, uncertainty may justify a reversible precaution while evidence is gathered; it does not justify inventing facts about blame.'],
 ['Evaluate real options','Compare feasible courses of action using authority, rights, harms, fairness and practical capacity. The best option is often a sequence rather than a single dramatic act: protect people, secure evidence, obtain appropriate approval, communicate and review. Avoid presenting an obviously illegal or cruel option as equally reasonable merely to fill a table.'],
 ['Finish with implementation','Name the responsible role, immediate action, documentation, communication and follow-up. Consider people excluded by the proposed remedy. Emotional intelligence helps maintain composure and recognise others’ concerns, but should not become manipulation. A good case answer ends with accountable implementation and learning, not a promise to solve every structural problem instantly.']
 ],[['Immediate response','Prevent urgent harm','Use proportionate and authorised steps.'],['Fact-finding','Establish what happened','Do not confuse allegations with findings.'],['Communication','Explain action and uncertainty','Protect privacy and avoid unsupported blame.'],['Follow-up','Review outcomes and correct weaknesses','A decision is not the end of responsibility.']], [['Proportionality','A response suited to the legitimate objective and level of risk.'],['Due process','Fair procedures before consequential decisions.'],['Emotional intelligence','Recognising and managing emotion in oneself and interactions.']], [['Protect','Address immediate risks and vulnerable people.'],['Verify','Secure information and use fair procedures.'],['Follow through','Communicate, document and review.']], 'An ethical answer needs a feasible course of action, not only a list of admirable values.', 'Relief supplies are limited and an influential person demands priority for their locality. How should you respond?', 'Use transparent need-based criteria, explain them consistently, document pressure, and seek additional supplies through proper channels. Protect urgent cases without allowing influence to replace evidence, and provide a route for overlooked needs to be assessed.', null)
 ]
};

export const ethicsTopics = [
 [
 ['Ethics and everyday judgment','Ethical reasoning asks whether conduct can be justified to the people affected, not only whether it is convenient. Begin by separating the facts of a situation from your assumptions, then identify the values at stake. A familiar habit or popular view can still need scrutiny.','Describe a decision in which convenience conflicts with a duty.'],
 ['Values and their formation','Families, schools, peers and institutions influence values, but individuals can reflect on inherited beliefs. A value becomes meaningful when it shapes conduct under pressure. Distinguish the values a person announces from the incentives and behaviour an organisation actually rewards.','Contrast a stated organisational value with an incentive that could undermine it.'],
 ['Duty and consequences','Consequences matter, but an attractive result does not automatically justify deception or disregard for rights. Duties also need interpretation in context. Compare the strongest reasons for an option, the constraints on it and the risks of using the same justification in future cases.','Apply duty and consequence reasoning to withholding information.'],
 ['Virtue and practical wisdom','Virtues are dispositions developed through practice, reflection and judgment. Courage differs from recklessness; compassion differs from arbitrary favour. A responsible response depends on understanding the circumstances and choosing a defensible action, rather than mechanically maximising one quality.','Explain courage using one cautious but firm public-service action.'],
 ['Empathy and compassion','Empathy helps understand another person’s situation; compassion adds concern and a wish to respond to suffering. Neither requires abandoning evidence or fair criteria. Listening respectfully can reveal barriers invisible in a file, while a recorded decision preserves equal treatment and accountability.','Show how empathy can improve a service without creating favouritism.'],
 ['Attitude and persuasion','Attitudes involve beliefs, feelings and tendencies to act. Persuasion can use evidence, trust and dialogue; manipulation suppresses meaningful choice or exploits vulnerability. A public campaign should be understandable, truthful and sensitive to its audience, with opportunities to ask questions.','Design a truthful public message and explain what would make it manipulative.'],
 ['Emotional intelligence','Recognising anger or anxiety can help an official respond calmly rather than defensively. Self-regulation does not mean suppressing every emotion, and understanding others does not justify controlling them covertly. Connect emotional awareness to listening, de-escalation and a fair decision.','Describe how you would respond to an angry but eligible applicant.'],
 ['Means and ends','Methods shape trust, institutions and future incentives. A shortcut that appears to produce a useful result can damage the process on which other people rely. Examine authorised discretion and legitimate alternatives before treating procedure as an obstacle that may simply be ignored.','Explain a case where a good objective is undermined by the method used.']
 ],[
 ['Integrity under pressure','Integrity is tested when a justified commitment becomes costly. An officer who records an inconvenient fact preserves the basis for a sound decision even if a superior dislikes it. Respectful communication and proper escalation can combine professional discipline with refusal to falsify evidence.','Draft a short response to a request to alter an inspection finding.'],
 ['Impartiality and equity','Fairness uses relevant criteria consistently; it does not require ignoring different needs. Emergency relief can prioritise severe vulnerability under transparent criteria without becoming partisan. Explain both the rule and the reason for a justified distinction so that others can scrutinise it.','Distinguish need-based priority from preferential treatment.'],
 ['Non-partisanship','A public servant serves lawful public institutions rather than a political party. Respect for elected authority is compatible with accurate advice and refusal to misuse official resources. The appropriate response to pressure should preserve records, use authorised channels and avoid personal political campaigning in an official role.','Identify the difference between implementing a lawful policy and campaigning for a party.'],
 ['Conflict of interest','A personal connection or financial interest can create a risk of biased judgment even without an improper decision. Early disclosure allows the institution to manage that risk. Recusal or independent review may be appropriate; concealing the connection deprives others of the chance to assess fairness.','Explain why disclosure is needed before proof of wrongdoing.'],
 ['Transparency and privacy','Public scrutiny requires information, but disclosure can also harm people when unnecessary personal details are exposed. Identify the purpose of disclosure and release what is justified through the applicable process. Accountability does not require publishing every private fact in an administrative file.','Suggest a way to publish service performance while protecting applicants’ identities.'],
 ['Citizen charters and redress','A service commitment is useful when it specifies standards, responsibility and a complaint route. A grievance mechanism should be accessible and lead to review or correction. Counting complaints closed without checking whether the problem was resolved can reward superficial compliance.','Propose a better measure than the number of complaints marked closed.'],
 ['Work culture and incentives','Rules influence conduct, but so do informal expectations, leadership and rewards. Unrealistic targets can encourage false reporting; unpunished harassment can silence dissent. An ethical response combines individual responsibility with changes to supervision, protection and incentives that make good conduct sustainable.','Explain how a target can unintentionally encourage dishonest reporting.'],
 ['Probity in public spending','Public spending requires a justified purpose, fair selection, records and verification of delivery. The lowest quoted price is not always the lowest total cost, but discretion must be transparent and within the rules. Separate a reasoned quality criterion from an excuse to favour a particular supplier.','List the evidence needed to justify a procurement choice.']
 ],[
 ['Relief allocation','When supplies are scarce, identify urgent need, vulnerability and available alternatives. Publish understandable criteria and create a way for overlooked cases to be assessed. Pressure from influential groups should be documented and addressed through proper channels rather than allowed to replace evidence.','Allocate scarce relief using three defensible criteria.'],
 ['Exam integrity','An allegation of a paper leak requires protecting evidence and the examination’s credibility while avoiding unsupported accusations. Separate urgent containment from findings of guilt. Fair inquiry, communication and a reasoned decision on the affected process protect candidates as well as institutional trust.','Distinguish immediate safeguards from punishment after an inquiry.'],
 ['Public misinformation','A harmful rumour requires timely verified information, coordination and proportionate action. Do not amplify private details or falsely claim certainty. Explain what is known, what is being checked and how people can obtain help, while ensuring that vulnerable groups are not unfairly blamed.','Write the three elements of a responsible initial public statement.'],
 ['Whistleblowing and evidence','Concerns about misconduct should be recorded accurately and raised through appropriate protected channels where available. Distinguish direct evidence from suspicion, minimise unnecessary disclosure and assess risks to affected people. A responsible account seeks correction without inventing facts or using allegations as personal retaliation.','Explain why preserving evidence differs from publicly circulating an allegation.'],
 ['Digital exclusion','A service can become inaccessible when applicants lack connectivity, literacy or reliable authentication. Preserve legitimate checks while providing authorised assistance and alternatives. Treating every failure as applicant fault hides a design problem and can deny entitlements to the people the programme was meant to serve.','Design a fair fallback for a failed online application.'],
 ['Environmental trade-offs','A project may promise jobs while imposing environmental and livelihood costs. Identify affected groups, evidence, legal processes and alternatives. Consultation should be meaningful, not merely a signature sheet. A decision needs reasons, safeguards, monitoring and remedies rather than a slogan that growth or conservation must always win.','Compare two feasible alternatives to a project rather than only approval or rejection.'],
 ['Team misconduct','A colleague’s error and deliberate falsification require different responses. Protect anyone at risk, preserve relevant records and use a fair process to establish facts. Loyalty to a team does not require concealing harm; a supportive culture can encourage reporting and correction without prejudging allegations.','Separate immediate correction, fact-finding and disciplinary decisions.'],
 ['The complete case answer','A strong case answer identifies the decision, stakeholders and value conflict, evaluates realistic options and sets out implementation. Give a reasoned recommendation with documentation, communication and review. Avoid an ending that promises instant structural reform without authority, resources or a practical sequence.','Write a complete answer using facts, options, choice, action and review.']
 ]
];
